\chapter{Polymorphism and specialization}
\label{ch:15}
\index{Polymorphism and specialization}
\index{Type inference!specialization}

The implemented polymorphic profile generalizes eligible immutable values under a value restriction and elaborates supported top-level and explicit universal forms. Different uses can request different concrete instances of a generalized function. A mutable binding keeps one type. Recursive inference and polymorphic recursion need separate consideration because a recursive call can otherwise demand an unbounded sequence of instances.

\OnxInputCode[firstline=1,lastline=9]{../../on1x/examples/destructuring_polymorphism.on1x}
Here a tuple pattern introduces independent leaves. The function leaf can be instantiated for both an integer and a Boolean. The Boolean leaf is already concrete. Elaborated C11 has concrete functions and data layouts, so a value restriction or a specialization limit can reject source that would require a runtime universal representation. For an inference regression, test two concrete uses as well as a same-type repeat. Compare the generated symbols or inspected types to confirm the two uses were specialized without leaking a previous constraint.

\section{Which parts of a specialization may change?}
Follow the second tuple in the destructuring example. Its function
returns a pair consisting of the captured number and its own argument.
The number was 42 at the function's definition. Inside the later block
another binding named number has value zero. Project the function from
the earlier tuple and apply it first to text and then to false. The
two result types differ in their second component, but the first
component in \emph{both} results must still be 42. Specializing at the
type required by a later use must not perform name lookup for the
capture at that later location. The final assertion that the inner
number remains zero checks a third, independent binding.

For contrast, put an identity function into an inferred mutable local.
Use it once with an integer and then try to use it with a Boolean.
The first use fixes the parameter and result of this local to the
integer type. The second use cannot demand a fresh instance; a mutable
slot must be able to hold replacements of its chosen type. Making the
lambda body perfectly general is therefore insufficient. Both the body
and the way its value is bound matter. Even an immutable binding with
a call as initializer does not get the same generalization as the
nonexpansive lambda in the example.

